        <name>
          <![CDATA[A Train To Woking]]>
        </name>
        <description>
          <![CDATA[<p><i>Fluer De Lis Theatre Company</i> present&nbsp;&nbsp;<b style="font-size:large;">A Train To Woking</b></p><p><b><span>A dramatic comedy exploring the 19th-century London
Necropolis Railway (LNR) and the final journey of its deceased passengers from
Waterloo to Brookwood. Through a blend of vivid storytelling and physical
theatre, the production transforms an important piece of history into a
catalyst for conversations about mortality and the legacies we leave behind.</span></b></p>

<p><i><font size="3">It&rsquo;s
the 19th century and London is plagued with disease. The overcrowded graveyards
are unable accommodate the rate of fatality and the city needs a solution. As a
means of response, the deceased must be transported out of the city and to
their final resting place.&nbsp;</font></i></p><p><i><font size="3">With support
from South Western Railway, Brookwood Cemetery and The Woking &amp; Sam Beare
Hospice, Fleur de Lis Theatre presents &lsquo;A Train to Woking&rsquo;.&nbsp;</font></i></p><p><b>CAST:</b></p><p><font size="3">Allan Nicol&nbsp;- Conductor</font></p><p><font size="3">Caroline Lawrie - Ma&rsquo;am</font></p><p><font size="3">Roger Conneff - Sir</font></p><p><font size="3">Christian Reid - Mr</font></p><p><font size="3">Alice Frances - Miss</font></p><p>









</p><p><font size="3">Pia Broughan - Child</font></p><p><b>CREATIVES</b></p><p>Christian Reid - Writer &amp; Director&nbsp;</p><p>Megan Barnes-Rolf - Producer &amp; Artistic Director&nbsp;</p><p>Leo Night - Assistant Director&nbsp;</p><p>Susie Coutts - Movement Director&nbsp;</p><p>Lewis&nbsp;Wareham -
Technical Designer&nbsp;</p>]]>
        </description>
